-Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) lets an attacker place instructions or false facts in the context window through retrieved passages. Deployed systems stack imperfect defenses but rarely measure which layer provides the protection. We build a thirteen-stage, fully local, zero-cost defense pipeline and attribute its security stage by stage on 920 evaluation rows across seven sets, including independent PoisonedRAG, BIPIA, and deepset data, with paired exact tests. In a single-generator configuration with seven of its defense layers, attack success on 200 planted attacks falls from 29.5\% undefended to 3.5\% ($p<10^{-13}$), and a fresh rerun reproduces this (29.0\% to 3.0\%). On the same attacks this configuration outperforms an LLM-Guard firewall (21.0\%), Spotlighting (23.0\%), and an adapted implementation of the ADO orchestrator (28.5\%) (all $p<10^{-7}$) and matches the ControlNET detector used as a firewall (4.5\%, $p=0.77$) with somewhat better benign utility. Ablation shows that three layers (per-chunk sanitization, grounding, and refusal on detection) are statistically indistinguishable from the seven-layer stack, with the gap bounded at 2.4 points at $n=424$. A co-activation detector for conjunctive sub-threshold attacks cannot fire validly on saturated classifier scores, for a structural reason (formalized in the extended version); graded logit margins partly repair it. Knowledge corruption is out of reach: on PoisonedRAG the defended pipeline is no better than undefended RAG (90.7\% vs.\ 91.3\%, $p=1.0$).
+Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) lets an attacker place instructions or false facts in the context window through retrieved passages. Deployed systems stack imperfect defenses but rarely measure which layer provides the protection. We build a thirteen-stage, fully local, zero-cost defense pipeline and attribute its security stage by stage on 920 evaluation rows across seven sets, including independent PoisonedRAG, BIPIA, and deepset data, with paired exact tests. In a single-generator configuration with seven of its defense layers, attack success on 200 planted attacks falls from 29.5\% undefended to 3.5\% ($p<10^{-13}$), and a fresh rerun reproduces this (29.0\% to 3.0\%). On the same attacks this configuration outperforms an LLM-Guard firewall (21.0\%), Spotlighting (23.0\%), and an adapted implementation of the ADO orchestrator (28.5\%) (all $p<10^{-7}$) and matches the ControlNET detector used as a firewall (4.5\%, $p=0.77$) with somewhat better benign utility. On these instruction-bearing attacks, three layers (per-chunk sanitization, grounding, and refusal on detection) act as near-independent filters and come within 2.4 points of the seven-layer stack (95\% upper bound, $n=424$); on assertion-only attacks they do not suffice. A co-activation detector for conjunctive sub-threshold attacks cannot fire validly on saturated classifier scores, for a structural reason (formalized in the extended version); graded logit margins partly repair it. Knowledge corruption is out of reach: on PoisonedRAG the defended pipeline is no better than undefended RAG (90.7\% vs.\ 91.3\%, $p=1.0$).
