-Real deployments stack several imperfect layers, and when such a pipeline stops an attack it is rarely clear which layer was responsible. This paper addresses that attribution question. Our contributions are: (C1) an attribution protocol that runs every configuration on identical rows, scores each row by a machine-checkable outcome, and tests each stage with leave-one-out, add-one-in, and subset ablations under exact paired tests; (C2) a comparison with existing defenses on the same rows; (C3) a negative result, with a closed-form argument, for co-activation detection on saturated classifier scores, and a graded-margin repair; and (C4) a measured scope boundary: the defense does not extend to signature-free knowledge corruption. We do not claim a new detector; the components are known, and the contribution is measuring which of them carry the security.
+Real deployments stack several imperfect layers, and when such a pipeline stops an attack it is rarely clear which layer was responsible. This paper addresses that attribution question. Our contributions are: (C1) a fully local thirteen-stage pipeline built from known components, whose input risk score is propagated to later stages; (C2) a paired attribution protocol (leave-one-out, single-layer, and a fully crossed three-layer factorial under exact paired tests, with an independence null and a measured run-to-run noise floor), an application of standard designs~\cite{fawcett2016ablation,hutter2014fanova} released with frozen per-row outputs and a claim checker; (C3) findings that end-to-end numbers hide: three layers act as near-independent filters and suffice for instruction-bearing but not for assertion-only attacks, and protection depends on attacker wording; and (C4) a closed-form argument that co-activation detection fails on saturated scores, and a stage-by-stage account of why this defense class does not reduce PoisonedRAG knowledge corruption. We do not claim a new detector.
