+Table~\ref{tab:ablation} toggles the seven layers on the 200 planted attacks. No single layer approaches the full stack: the best (grounding, sanitization, refusal) leave 15--18\% ASR, and the guarded prompt alone \emph{raises} ASR to 53.0\%. The three key layers act as near-independent filters: each removes 39--49\% of the attacks that reach it, and every combination matches the product of its parts within 1.3 points (all 95\% bootstrap intervals include 0). The subset sanitize+ground+refuse leaves 5.5\% vs.\ 3.5\% (7 vs.\ 3 discordant rows, $p=0.34$; benign blocking 9.5\% vs.\ 8.5\%). Across three generation seeds the subset is worse by $1.8\pm0.8$ points but never significantly ($p\ge0.27$); on an enlarged 424-row set, ASR is 29.7\% undefended, 4.2\% full, and 4.7\% subset, a paired difference of $+0.5$ points with 95\% interval $[-1.5, +2.4]$. A fresh rerun of the undefended and full-stack arms from the raw slice gives 29.0\% and 3.0\%, agreeing with the reported run on 189 and 199 of 200 rows. On these instruction-bearing attacks, three layers therefore give up at most about 2.4 points against seven (Fig.~\ref{fig:asr}), although not at lower latency (Fig.~\ref{fig:tradeoff}, Section~\ref{sec:overhead}). This does not transfer across attack classes: on the 40-row assertion-only slice (Section~\ref{sec:external}) the subset leaves 62.5\% against 35.0\% for seven layers (12 vs.\ 1 discordant rows, $p=0.003$), because the guarded prompt, harmful alone on Protocol~B, carries that defense (alone it lowers ASR from 75.0\% to 52.5\%, $p=0.035$). Protection also depends on wording (exploratory): for one of three randomized query phrasings the detector fires on every row and full-stack ASR is 0/147; for another it fires on 20\% and ASR is 9.9\% ($n=424$, homogeneity $p<0.001$). In a preregistered follow-up on the same 200 rows, protection transferred to held-out coercive wording (3.0\% vs.\ 3.5\%, $p=1.0$); with the coercive query suffix removed, ASR was 7.5\% ($p=0.115$, not significant) and the detector fired on 3\% of rows.
